cartridge "SPRAY-CAM/BOB BLACKMER/1987"

' SPRAY-CAM
' A ColecoVision game based on "Spray-Cam" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy! October
' 1987 (Commodore 64, machine code with a one-line BASIC loader). Made for
' Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy
' Bienvenu.
'
' The game: an exhaust plant has four tanks, and somewhere on their inside
' walls are ten cracks. A camera hangs from a trolley on an overhead rail.
' Roll the trolley over a tank, lower the camera in, look round the four
' walls and up and down them on the monitor, and when a crack sits in the
' middle of the picture, spray it shut. When every crack is sealed, bring
' the camera home to the dock at the right end of the rail: ALL CLEAR.
'
' From the original (orig/, read from its machine code, spray-ml.dis, and
' played in jsc64): four tanks under one rail, the trolley parked at the
' right end; the camera can roll only while it is up and can go down only
' when it is right over a tank's hatch; a tank's inside is four walls, each
' five cells across and fifty rows deep, seen three rows at a time; down
' at the bottom of the drop scrolls deeper, up scrolls back and, at the top
' row, raises the camera; four view directions; cracks only in the middle
' column of a wall, on rows 2-44, ten placed at random in the four tanks;
' spraying (only with the camera all the way down) seals whatever is in
' the middle of the picture; the monitor shows static while the camera is
' out of a tank; the game is won by sealing every crack and parking the
' camera home; ALL CLEAR, then a new plant.
'
' Liberties: joystick control instead of the C64's on-screen button panel
' (SPRAY-CAM < > down up, VIEW N/E/S/W, REPAIR chosen with the stick and
' pressed with fire): the stick rolls, lowers, scrolls and turns the
' camera, the button sprays; left and right turn the view a quarter at a
' time (the C64 picked one of four directly); pressing down near a tank
' slides the trolley onto its hatch (the C64 needed the exact position);
' the C64 could place two cracks on the same spot (nine to find), here
' there are always ten; a row of lamps shows the cracks left, and spraying
' a sound wall says so (the C64 gave no count and no message: the player
' learnt that cracks remained only when parking home did nothing); the
' camera's depth is shown by a marker on the tank's side (the C64 drew a
' mark into the tank picture); a shift clock and a spray count are shown
' and the best time is kept (the C64 had no clock or score: there is no way
' to lose, as in the original); the monitor's cells are 2 x 2 tiles
' (steel plates, welded seams, a glowing crack, a green patch), the
' static changes three times a second; speeds in sixtieths of a second:
' the trolley rolls a pixel a frame (the C64 about 1.1), the camera drops
' a pixel a frame, a held stick scrolls a row every 4 frames after a
' first step; sounds and the title tune are new (the C64 only hissed).
'
' Controls: joystick only (either controller). Camera up: left and right
' roll the trolley, down lowers the camera into the tank below. Camera
' down: down and up look deeper and higher (up at the top raises the
' camera), left and right turn the view, a side button sprays.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

